% ============================================================================
% sedimentary-rock-formation-step.query.adj — a worked recall over the
% earth-science sedimentary-rock-formation-step library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "which step of
% sedimentary rock formation is erosion?" (direct binding) or "which stage
% comes third?" (reverse binding) — it states no earth-science fact of its
% own, it only `import`s the grounded table and asks binding queries. The
% third query runs the relation BACKWARD, binding the step number and
% recalling the stage. The final two queries demonstrate honest abstention:
% `deposition` is a real term OTHER K-8 sources use for a stage between
% erosion and compaction, but the cited page's own paragraph never names it
% as its own numbered stage; `melting` is a real term the SAME cited page
% uses, but only for the separate igneous-rock path, not this sedimentary
% one.
%
% Run (from this directory so the relative import resolves):
%     ../../../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli sedimentary-rock-formation-step.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "sedimentary-rock-formation-step.adj"

? sedimentary_rock_formation_step(weathering, $N)    % expect 1 (the process starts here)
? sedimentary_rock_formation_step(erosion, $N)        % expect 2
? sedimentary_rock_formation_step($S, 3)              % expect compaction (reverse)
? sedimentary_rock_formation_step(deposition, $N)     % expect abstain -- the cited
                                                       %   page never names this as
                                                       %   its own numbered stage
? sedimentary_rock_formation_step(melting, $N)        % expect abstain -- a real term
                                                       %   the same page uses, but only
                                                       %   for the separate igneous path
